1
00:00:00,690 --> 00:00:03,000
Superintelligence is arriving.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:04,750
That's what Meta's AI chief

3
00:00:04,790 --> 00:00:06,100
announced this weekend.

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:07,360
Is he right?

5
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:08,680
Because if he is,

6
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,590
the biggest change

7
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:10,460
of your lifetime

8
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:11,950
has already begun.

9
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:14,200
His evidence is a single file,

10
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:15,510
published days ago.

11
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:17,420
In it, a mathematician working

12
00:00:17,460 --> 00:00:18,580
with an AI claims

13
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:19,550
a proof of the most

14
00:00:19,590 --> 00:00:20,810
famous unsolved

15
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:22,310
problem in mathematics:

16
00:00:22,370 --> 00:00:23,910
the Riemann hypothesis.

17
00:00:24,190 --> 00:00:25,050
A separate program

18
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:26,030
checked that proof,

19
00:00:26,070 --> 00:00:27,250
step by step.

20
00:00:27,390 --> 00:00:29,450
Its verdict: correct.

21
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:31,980
But written out in plain language,

22
00:00:32,020 --> 00:00:33,540
it runs to over a quarter

23
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:35,210
of a million pages.

24
00:00:35,480 --> 00:00:37,050
So far, no human

25
00:00:37,090 --> 00:00:38,500
fully understands it.

26
00:00:38,690 --> 00:00:39,890
Not even the mathematician

27
00:00:39,930 --> 00:00:40,960
who published it.

28
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:42,940
Meta's AI chief says

29
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:44,660
it was "completed with the help

30
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:46,040
of an alien mind

31
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:47,750
that we don't understand."

32
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:49,960
Many physicists, he writes,

33
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,510
would call the atomic bomb

34
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:53,560
a homework problem next to this.

35
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:54,960
If he's right,

36
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:57,250
this is what arrival looks like.

37
00:00:57,530 --> 00:00:58,380
It's not arriving

38
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,020
as a machine we fear.

39
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:01,790
It's arriving as an answer

40
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:03,250
we can't read.

41
00:01:03,790 --> 00:01:05,440
So test his claim.

42
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:06,720
Five checks.

43
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:08,400
He doesn't pass them all.

44
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:11,440
The man is Alexandr Wang,

45
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:13,400
Meta's chief AI officer.

46
00:01:13,590 --> 00:01:14,260
He runs a lab

47
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:15,750
with the goal in its name:

48
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:17,920
Meta Superintelligence Labs.

49
00:01:18,130 --> 00:01:19,520
That bomb comparison?

50
00:01:19,680 --> 00:01:21,000
He gives no source for it.

51
00:01:21,290 --> 00:01:22,470
And still, his essay

52
00:01:22,510 --> 00:01:23,990
is no victory lap.

53
00:01:24,050 --> 00:01:25,130
He calls it "lazy

54
00:01:25,170 --> 00:01:26,120
thinking" to treat

55
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:28,150
superintelligence as inevitable.

56
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:30,150
But look at who is speaking.

57
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:31,580
Building superintelligence

58
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:32,790
is Wang's job,

59
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:33,800
and his essay also

60
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:35,240
promotes his own lab.

61
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:36,970
Here he is, months earlier,

62
00:01:37,010 --> 00:01:38,520
describing that project.

63
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:40,300
A lot of the leading labs,

64
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:42,600
they build the entire

65
00:01:42,640 --> 00:01:44,040
organizations around the premise

66
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:47,600
that superintelligence is coming

67
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:49,320
and it is very close.

68
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:51,060
And yet this proof

69
00:01:51,100 --> 00:01:52,570
came from a competitor.

70
00:01:52,820 --> 00:01:54,670
Maybe he means every word.

71
00:01:54,940 --> 00:01:55,690
It also helps

72
00:01:55,730 --> 00:01:57,290
him: if superintelligence

73
00:01:57,330 --> 00:01:59,860
is arriving, his lab matters more.

74
00:02:00,050 --> 00:02:01,230
Both can be true.

75
00:02:01,520 --> 00:02:03,460
So his word is not enough.

76
00:02:03,770 --> 00:02:05,460
Check the claim itself.

77
00:02:06,630 --> 00:02:09,420
First check: is his evidence real?

78
00:02:09,660 --> 00:02:10,490
What was published

79
00:02:10,530 --> 00:02:11,580
is a short note,

80
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:13,180
and about 17 million

81
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:14,300
lines of code.

82
00:02:14,490 --> 00:02:16,010
No human reads that.

83
00:02:16,050 --> 00:02:18,110
Not you, not the man who wrote it.

84
00:02:18,320 --> 00:02:19,890
It was written for a referee:

85
00:02:19,930 --> 00:02:21,260
a program called Lean,

86
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,120
which checks every logical step

87
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:25,030
and can't be talked into anything.

88
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:27,420
The referee's ruling: correct.

89
00:02:27,650 --> 00:02:28,900
Within a day, the people who

90
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:30,860
build that referee were convinced.

91
00:02:31,060 --> 00:02:32,620
At MIT, an independent

92
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:33,660
check is running.

93
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:35,050
Most runs have passed.

94
00:02:35,090 --> 00:02:36,500
A few are still open.

95
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,010
First box: gold.

96
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:41,030
The evidence is real,

97
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:42,970
and so far, it holds.

98
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:44,790
And if it keeps holding?

99
00:02:45,170 --> 00:02:47,000
One mathematician's answer:

100
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:48,480
So, the Riemann hypothesis

101
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:50,420
is sometimes dubbed

102
00:02:50,460 --> 00:02:52,160
the most famous and most important

103
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:53,920
problem in mathematics,

104
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:55,420
and it's describing

105
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:58,300
the distribution of prime numbers.

106
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:00,630
But a referee only rules

107
00:03:00,670 --> 00:03:02,130
on the game it is given.

108
00:03:02,530 --> 00:03:04,320
Check two: does the code

109
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:05,810
prove the right thing?

110
00:03:06,040 --> 00:03:07,430
One critic's worry:

111
00:03:07,660 --> 00:03:08,560
the code may prove

112
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,630
a sentence that is

113
00:03:09,670 --> 00:03:11,100
true by definition.

114
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:12,190
Something that sounds

115
00:03:12,230 --> 00:03:14,170
grand and says nothing.

116
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:15,350
But the author didn't

117
00:03:15,390 --> 00:03:16,500
write the question.

118
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:18,310
The Riemann hypothesis already

119
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:20,230
stood in the referee's rule book,

120
00:03:20,270 --> 00:03:21,860
put there by someone else.

121
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:23,040
The people who build

122
00:03:23,080 --> 00:03:24,700
the referee reject it.

123
00:03:24,970 --> 00:03:26,330
The argument goes on.

124
00:03:26,690 --> 00:03:28,650
Second box: gold,

125
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:30,040
with a question mark.

126
00:03:31,150 --> 00:03:32,870
But whose work is it?

127
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:36,470
Check three: was it really the AI?

128
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:37,670
The proof comes

129
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:38,810
from a mathematician

130
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:40,290
at an AI company,

131
00:03:40,330 --> 00:03:41,930
working with its AI.

132
00:03:42,150 --> 00:03:43,390
The problem is older

133
00:03:43,430 --> 00:03:44,640
than the light bulb.

134
00:03:44,890 --> 00:03:45,840
The checkable proof

135
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,600
took about 6 weeks.

136
00:03:47,780 --> 00:03:49,120
That company gains

137
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:51,090
if its AI gets the credit.

138
00:03:51,310 --> 00:03:52,100
So listen to

139
00:03:52,140 --> 00:03:53,760
the mathematician himself.

140
00:03:54,080 --> 00:03:55,430
He doesn't fully understand

141
00:03:55,470 --> 00:03:56,790
the proof he published.

142
00:03:57,100 --> 00:03:59,040
Publishing it anyway, he said,

143
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:00,880
is "frankly completely

144
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:01,990
outrageous."

145
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:03,420
You don't say that about

146
00:04:03,460 --> 00:04:04,660
your own idea.

147
00:04:05,050 --> 00:04:06,940
Third box: gold,

148
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:08,430
on his own account.

149
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:11,250
Compare that with a second proof,

150
00:04:11,290 --> 00:04:13,160
claimed on the very same day.

151
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:14,380
By a human.

152
00:04:14,730 --> 00:04:17,230
So far, that one is still a claim.

153
00:04:17,370 --> 00:04:18,140
The machine's proof

154
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:19,030
has been checked,

155
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:20,640
and nobody can read it.

156
00:04:21,810 --> 00:04:24,120
That leaves Wang's boldest phrase:

157
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,820
the "alien mind".

158
00:04:26,410 --> 00:04:27,910
Check four: does any

159
00:04:27,950 --> 00:04:29,450
human understand it?

160
00:04:29,840 --> 00:04:31,100
On the day it appeared,

161
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:31,940
the mathematician

162
00:04:31,980 --> 00:04:33,580
Terence Tao wrote:

163
00:04:33,790 --> 00:04:35,060
"the digestion process

164
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,480
will take some time."

165
00:04:36,790 --> 00:04:37,760
Another mathematician

166
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:38,900
said it bluntly:

167
00:04:39,190 --> 00:04:41,150
no one understands the proof.

168
00:04:41,470 --> 00:04:43,420
Here is Tao, in an earlier talk,

169
00:04:43,460 --> 00:04:45,720
on proofs checked by machines.

170
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:47,140
It hasn't quite happened yet,

171
00:04:47,180 --> 00:04:48,700
but we are very, very close

172
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:50,100
to a scenario in which

173
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,360
a major result gets proved

174
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:53,560
and verified, and no human

175
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:55,270
can understand, can explain it.

176
00:04:55,870 --> 00:04:57,930
So "correct" is not

177
00:04:57,970 --> 00:04:59,640
the same as understood.

178
00:04:59,900 --> 00:05:00,930
The referee can tell you

179
00:05:00,970 --> 00:05:02,610
every step was legal.

180
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:03,590
It can't tell you

181
00:05:03,630 --> 00:05:05,100
why the game was won.

182
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:06,750
And that gap is not a problem

183
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:08,420
for mathematicians alone.

184
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:10,010
Sooner or later it arrives

185
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:12,470
at your table, stamped correct.

186
00:05:12,710 --> 00:05:14,800
This box may never turn gold.

187
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:16,080
A quarter of a million

188
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:17,110
pages is more

189
00:05:17,150 --> 00:05:18,170
than one person can

190
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:19,560
read in a lifetime.

191
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:22,970
So on this point, Wang is right.

192
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:24,800
Something has arrived that reasons

193
00:05:24,840 --> 00:05:26,520
further than we can read.

194
00:05:27,090 --> 00:05:28,370
If you pictured the arrival

195
00:05:28,410 --> 00:05:29,940
as a loud moment:

196
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:32,040
no machine announced itself.

197
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:33,400
A human did.

198
00:05:33,770 --> 00:05:35,660
What arrived was quiet.

199
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:37,100
A single file.

200
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:39,290
This channel exists

201
00:05:39,330 --> 00:05:40,610
for one reason:

202
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:41,660
so that the arrival

203
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:42,830
of superintelligence

204
00:05:42,870 --> 00:05:44,660
goes well for everybody.

205
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:45,530
Not just for the people

206
00:05:45,570 --> 00:05:46,570
who build it.

207
00:05:46,770 --> 00:05:47,770
Want that too?

208
00:05:47,880 --> 00:05:48,890
Then subscribe.

209
00:05:50,060 --> 00:05:51,630
But Wang's headline doesn't

210
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:53,480
say a proof is arriving.

211
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:55,660
It says superintelligence.

212
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:57,060
So, the last check:

213
00:05:57,310 --> 00:05:58,460
does one proof mean

214
00:05:58,500 --> 00:05:59,910
superintelligence?

215
00:06:00,460 --> 00:06:02,650
James Maynard, a Fields medalist,

216
00:06:02,690 --> 00:06:03,490
calls the proof

217
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:05,150
a "historic milestone",

218
00:06:05,190 --> 00:06:06,320
if it is correct.

219
00:06:06,550 --> 00:06:08,480
But for him, little changes:

220
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:09,420
he never doubted

221
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:11,110
the hypothesis was true.

222
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:12,570
The sharpest doubt

223
00:06:12,610 --> 00:06:13,940
comes from Yann LeCun.

224
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:15,680
He left Meta a year ago,

225
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:16,830
and has called Wang

226
00:06:16,870 --> 00:06:18,170
"inexperienced".

227
00:06:18,550 --> 00:06:20,100
No neutral witness, then.

228
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:22,170
But his objection is technical.

229
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:24,390
We are not going to get

230
00:06:24,430 --> 00:06:24,970
to human-level AI

231
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:27,230
by just scaling up LLMs.

232
00:06:27,270 --> 00:06:29,320
This is just not going to happen.

233
00:06:29,920 --> 00:06:31,400
The kind of AI behind

234
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:33,600
today's chatbots, he argues,

235
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:34,580
is a dead end

236
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:36,620
on the way to superintelligence.

237
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:38,200
So the fifth box

238
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,960
only fills halfway.

239
00:06:40,390 --> 00:06:42,150
"Arriving" is a bet,

240
00:06:42,220 --> 00:06:43,020
placed by a man

241
00:06:43,060 --> 00:06:44,230
with a stake in it.

242
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,100
But the gap between correct

243
00:06:46,140 --> 00:06:48,190
and understood is no bet.

244
00:06:48,390 --> 00:06:49,590
It is already here.

245
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:51,370
And behind that headline,

246
00:06:51,410 --> 00:06:53,410
Wang's own essay is worried.

247
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:54,510
He finds it hard

248
00:06:54,550 --> 00:06:55,790
to imagine humanity

249
00:06:55,830 --> 00:06:57,550
keeping control of something "a

250
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:59,610
thousand times smarter and more

251
00:06:59,650 --> 00:07:00,960
capable than humans

252
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,350
in every field."

253
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:03,990
And he writes:

254
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:05,580
"We should not be okay

255
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:08,500
with a handover of society to AI."

256
00:07:08,790 --> 00:07:09,630
A handover.

257
00:07:09,670 --> 00:07:11,890
What would that look like?

258
00:07:13,070 --> 00:07:14,410
Not like a coup.

259
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:16,460
More like an ordinary evening,

260
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:17,670
a few years from now,

261
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:18,760
in your town hall.

262
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:20,840
The old bridge, the one you cross

263
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:23,130
every morning, has to be replaced.

264
00:07:23,310 --> 00:07:25,170
A system has designed the new one,

265
00:07:25,210 --> 00:07:26,430
and marked its own design

266
00:07:26,470 --> 00:07:28,650
with one word: correct.

267
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:29,790
The reasoning runs

268
00:07:29,830 --> 00:07:30,890
longer than anyone

269
00:07:30,930 --> 00:07:32,580
in the room could ever read.

270
00:07:32,750 --> 00:07:34,360
The engineers can't follow it.

271
00:07:34,410 --> 00:07:35,410
Neither can you.

272
00:07:35,530 --> 00:07:36,510
But the system has

273
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:37,700
never been wrong.

274
00:07:37,990 --> 00:07:39,490
So the hands go up.

275
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:40,640
Yours, too.

276
00:07:41,100 --> 00:07:42,740
That is a handover.

277
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:44,650
No robots in the street.

278
00:07:44,690 --> 00:07:46,410
Nobody seizing power.

279
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:48,700
Just a thousand small yeses.

280
00:07:48,900 --> 00:07:49,710
Your loan.

281
00:07:49,750 --> 00:07:50,730
Your contract.

282
00:07:50,770 --> 00:07:52,010
Your town's budget.

283
00:07:52,270 --> 00:07:53,670
Each one sensible.

284
00:07:53,830 --> 00:07:55,960
Each one a little less yours.

285
00:07:56,210 --> 00:07:56,780
But look at what

286
00:07:56,820 --> 00:07:58,980
the mathematicians actually did.

287
00:07:59,080 --> 00:07:59,770
They did not take

288
00:07:59,810 --> 00:08:00,880
the machine's word,

289
00:08:00,920 --> 00:08:02,110
or the company's.

290
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:03,480
They trusted a referee

291
00:08:03,520 --> 00:08:04,810
that humans built.

292
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:06,150
Its rules are open.

293
00:08:06,190 --> 00:08:07,670
Anyone can run it again.

294
00:08:08,290 --> 00:08:09,740
Their trust moved:

295
00:08:09,890 --> 00:08:11,210
away from the answer,

296
00:08:11,310 --> 00:08:12,310
to the check.

297
00:08:12,800 --> 00:08:13,790
In your town hall,

298
00:08:13,830 --> 00:08:16,110
the system was its own referee.

299
00:08:16,380 --> 00:08:18,090
In mathematics, humans

300
00:08:18,130 --> 00:08:19,720
built the referee first.

301
00:08:19,890 --> 00:08:21,740
So who builds the referees

302
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:23,120
for everything else?

303
00:08:24,280 --> 00:08:25,410
The labs could.

304
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:27,680
Wang himself writes that a better

305
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:29,610
future likely involves the whole

306
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:30,720
industry taking

307
00:08:30,760 --> 00:08:31,820
"meaningfully different

308
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:33,650
actions from the default path".

309
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:35,900
He doesn't say exactly how.

310
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:37,770
One way: labs agree

311
00:08:37,810 --> 00:08:38,620
that their strongest

312
00:08:38,660 --> 00:08:39,910
systems hand in work

313
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:41,380
a referee can check.

314
00:08:41,510 --> 00:08:43,970
Then no answer is its own referee.

315
00:08:44,220 --> 00:08:45,530
The objection: whoever

316
00:08:45,570 --> 00:08:47,250
waits falls behind.

317
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:48,450
So governments could

318
00:08:48,490 --> 00:08:49,200
make it a rule

319
00:08:49,240 --> 00:08:50,650
where the stakes are high.

320
00:08:50,720 --> 00:08:52,070
Then your town can refuse

321
00:08:52,110 --> 00:08:54,160
to vote on what nobody can check.

322
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:56,200
But who checks the checkers?

323
00:08:56,800 --> 00:08:58,800
Mathematics has one answer:

324
00:08:58,950 --> 00:09:00,360
an open referee,

325
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:01,910
carried by nonprofits,

326
00:09:01,950 --> 00:09:03,530
not by one company.

327
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:04,770
The engineer in the back

328
00:09:04,810 --> 00:09:06,320
row can run it herself,

329
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:07,680
and nobody has to trust

330
00:09:07,720 --> 00:09:09,300
whoever sells the answer.

331
00:09:09,670 --> 00:09:10,780
The hardest objection

332
00:09:10,820 --> 00:09:12,080
is the simplest:

333
00:09:12,250 --> 00:09:13,570
results matter more

334
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:14,820
than understanding.

335
00:09:15,070 --> 00:09:16,440
You already cross bridges

336
00:09:16,480 --> 00:09:18,070
you could never calculate.

337
00:09:18,360 --> 00:09:19,350
True.

338
00:09:19,390 --> 00:09:22,010
But until now, some human could.

339
00:09:22,270 --> 00:09:23,680
None of this is agreed,

340
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:24,960
and none of it is tested

341
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:26,070
at this scale.

342
00:09:26,290 --> 00:09:26,990
Who could change

343
00:09:27,030 --> 00:09:29,190
that: labs, governments,

344
00:09:29,230 --> 00:09:31,830
investors, and voters like you.

345
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:34,400
And Wang puts a clock on it.

346
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:36,250
"How humanity acts over

347
00:09:36,290 --> 00:09:37,730
the next year will have

348
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:39,430
great consequence on what

349
00:09:39,470 --> 00:09:41,260
future world we live in."

350
00:09:42,490 --> 00:09:44,770
Superintelligence is arriving.

351
00:09:45,110 --> 00:09:46,670
So, is he right?

352
00:09:47,010 --> 00:09:48,010
In part.

353
00:09:48,130 --> 00:09:49,640
Something has arrived:

354
00:09:49,740 --> 00:09:51,230
an answer that holds,

355
00:09:51,270 --> 00:09:53,640
so far, and that we can't read.

356
00:09:53,940 --> 00:09:55,500
Whether that is superintelligence

357
00:09:55,540 --> 00:09:56,830
remains his bet.

358
00:09:57,110 --> 00:09:58,150
And the box that is still

359
00:09:58,190 --> 00:10:00,190
open isn't about the machine.

360
00:10:00,290 --> 00:10:01,420
It's about us.

361
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:02,370
So when the next

362
00:10:02,410 --> 00:10:03,900
headline says an AI

363
00:10:03,940 --> 00:10:06,500
solved something, ask two things.

364
00:10:06,690 --> 00:10:08,350
Can any human follow it?

365
00:10:08,530 --> 00:10:10,320
And who says it's correct?

366
00:10:11,210 --> 00:10:12,910
We may never read every answer.

367
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:14,300
But who gets to say

368
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:16,820
"correct" has to stay human.

369
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:19,160
This channel exists

370
00:10:19,200 --> 00:10:20,040
so that the arrival

371
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:21,510
of superintelligence goes

372
00:10:21,550 --> 00:10:23,000
well for everybody.

373
00:10:23,270 --> 00:10:24,270
Want that too?

374
00:10:24,370 --> 00:10:25,370
Then subscribe.

